      "text": "To find the mean of the set \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\), we first need to determine the value of \\(n\\). The median of a set of five numbers is the third number when the set is ordered. Given that the median is 9, we can order the set as follows:\n\n\\[n, n+5, n+6, n+9, n+15\\]\n\nSince the median is the third number, it must be \\(n + 6\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[n + 6 = 9\\]\n\nSolving for \\(n\\), we get:\n\n\\[n = 9 - 6 = 3\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(n = 3\\), we can substitute this value back into the set to get the actual numbers:\n\n\\[3, 3+5, 3+6, 3+9, 3+15 = 3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\]\n\nNext, we find the mean of these numbers. The mean is the sum of the numbers divided by the number of elements in the set. The sum of the numbers is:\n\n\\[3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18 = 50\\]\n\nThere are 5 numbers in the set, so the mean is:\n\n\\[\\frac{50}{5} = 10\\]\n\nThus, the mean of the set is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
